% Forward-chaining saturation for LeanRDF: the untrusted search.
%
% Terms are interned by the Lean side: every RDF term is an int, a triple term too (its
% parts through rdf.tt-of). The semi-naive rounds are driven from Lean
% (LeanRDFElpi/Saturate.lean): each round is one query for every solution of rdf.step,
% the triples found so far are rdf.known P S O Id facts and those of the last round
% rdf.delta P S O Id facts, where Id is the fact's number (its place in the closure). The
% facts, the triple-term table and the guard tables are Lean builtins over hash indexes,
% attached per round, so the Elpi database holds only the rule clauses:
%
%   pred rdf.known   o:int, o:int, o:int, o:int.   % P S O Id, any argument may be bound
%   pred rdf.has     i:int.                        % some fact has the predicate P
%   pred rdf.delta   o:int, o:int, o:int, o:int.
%   pred rdf.tt-of   o:int, o:int, o:int, o:int.   % Z S P O: Z is the triple term <<( S P O )>>
%   pred rdf.is-cmp  i:int.                        % an rdf:_n IRI
%   pred rdf.is-str  i:int.                        % an xsd:string literal
%   pred rdf.is-lang i:int.                        % a language-tagged literal
%   pred rdf.is-dirlang i:int.                     % a directional language-tagged literal
%
%
% The rule clauses rdf.step K R are generated from the Lean rule list
% (LeanRDFElpi/Codegen.lean), one per rule and premise position: that premise comes from the
% last round. K is 1 + the number of that premise's predicate when it is a constant, and 0
% when it is a variable. A round asks rdf.step K R once for K = 0 and once for each
% predicate of the last round (separate queries, so that no choice point spans the round),
% and the first-argument index tries only the clauses that can start. A solution R names
% the clause, the fact numbers of the premises in body order, and the head, whose triple
% terms are built with rdf.mk and interned by the Lean side.

kind rdfn type.
type rdf.n int -> rdfn.
type rdf.mk rdfn -> rdfn -> rdfn -> rdfn.

kind rdfstep type.
type rdf.r int -> list int -> rdfn -> rdfn -> rdfn -> rdfstep.

pred rdf.step o:int, o:rdfstep.
